---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---
<BaseLayout title="Resources — Thomas Vezzani" description="Reusable software assets, templates and learning material that survived real work.">
  <div class="shell">
    <header class="page-intro">
      <div class="kicker">Resources</div>
      <h1 class="page-title">Useful enough to leave the workshop.</h1>
      <p>I do not want a shop full of invented templates. When something survives real use across my own projects, I can clean it up, explain it, and sell or give away the reusable part.</p>
    </header>
    <div class="resource-grid">
      <article class="resource-card">
        <div class="kicker">candidate / not for sale yet</div>
        <h3>AI project starter</h3>
        <p>A cleaned-up version of the work-order, Builder/Reviewer, project-state and session-close machinery I use in real repositories.</p>
        <div class="meta">agents · git · execution</div>
      </article>
      <article class="resource-card">
        <div class="kicker">candidate / not for sale yet</div>
        <h3>Local AI starter</h3>
        <p>Grounding, retrieval, confidence gates and local-model seams derived from systems like Lexine, huhu and motes—not a toy chatbot template.</p>
        <div class="meta">local AI · RAG · abstention</div>
      </article>
      <article class="resource-card">
        <div class="kicker">candidate / not for sale yet</div>
        <h3>Product film kit</h3>
        <p>Reusable Remotion composition patterns for typography, UI mockups, motion grammar, localized strings and regenerable launch films.</p>
        <div class="meta">remotion · motion · product</div>
      </article>
    </div>
    <div class="section filter-note">Nothing here is purchasable yet. The threshold is simple: I should have used the thing enough myself that I would still recommend it after the novelty wore off.</div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
